:name: STM32F4 miniblink dynamic test
:description: Loads the miniblink firmware under test and leaves the monitor open on a TCP port so an outside script can poll GPIOD's ODR register directly to observe (or fail to observe) LED toggling.

# Run this with:
#   renode --disable-gui -P 4567 miniblink_test.resc &
# Then, from another terminal:
#   python3 led_probe.py
#
# -P 4567 puts Renode's own monitor console on a TCP port instead of an
# interactive prompt attached to this shell's stdin - without it, Renode
# exits immediately when run in the background (no terminal to read from).
# --disable-gui skips the GUI entirely, which this headless test doesn't need.
#
# Unlike usart_irq_test.resc, there's no UART bridge here - miniblink has no
# serial peripheral at all. Instead, led_probe.py polls GPIOD's own ODR
# (output data register, offset 0x14 from GPIOD's base 0x40020C00, per
# platforms/cpus/stm32f4.repl in this Renode install) directly over the
# monitor connection, checking bit 12 (the LED pin) across several reads.

using sysbus
mach create "miniblink_test"

# stm32f4_discovery.repl ships with Renode itself (Antmicro's official
# platform description for the real ST STM32F4-Discovery board). It wires
# GPIOD pin 12 to a genuine Miscellaneous.LED model (UserLED), not a stub.
# See recon/PLATFORM.md for how that was confirmed.
machine LoadPlatformDescription @platforms/boards/stm32f4_discovery.repl

# Absolute path to the actual compiled firmware under test. Re-seed a
# defect, rebuild, and this same script picks up the new .elf automatically.
$elf?=@/Users/gowthamreddys/Desktop/hydron-evaluation/stm32-pilot/firmware/libopencm3-examples/examples/stm32/f4/stm32f4-discovery/miniblink/miniblink.elf

sysbus LoadELF $elf

# Actually starts CPU execution - without this, the ELF is loaded but the
# emulated core never runs, and GPIOD's ODR would never change either way.
start
